00:00:05.213 --> 00:00:06.380
LUCY D'AGOSTINO MCGOWAN: Hi.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.240
I'm Lucy D'Agostino McGowan.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.820
And I'm excited to talk
to you about designing

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.400
randomized studies using shiny.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.240
You can find me on Twitter
@LucyStats and you can find

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.893
these slides at
lucymcgowan.com/talk.

00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:23.310
So the work I'm
going to be talking

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.770
about today was done in
collaboration with students

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:28.890
in my data science lab at
Wake Forest University,

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.950
in particular Jonathan
Trattner and Nuri Park.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.110
And you can find some
of our other projects

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.850
at dmds.lucymcgowan.com.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:39.050
OK.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.420
So I'm going to show you
four ways we can leverage

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.570
the power of Shiny to help
streamline our randomized study

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.990
design via informed
consent, randomization,

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:53.100
collecting demographics, and
incorporating learnr modules.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.390
So informed consent: informed
consent is a common process

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.120
when involving human
subjects in a trial.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:03.300
Often you hand people a piece of
paper and they either say that

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.695
they agree to be in a
study or they don't.

00:01:05.695 --> 00:01:07.320
And so instead of
that, we can actually

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.730
have the entire
process completed

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:12.150
online from the comfort of
your participants' browsers.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.460
And so we're basically going
to leverage Shiny, such

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.610
that if the user consents,
they're sent to the study.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:23.330
And if they don't, then they're
sent back to the home page.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:26.780
And so in Shiny, we often work
with two components: the user

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:27.650
interface--

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.990
what the user sees--
and the server--

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.240
what runs behind
the scenes in R.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.010
And so here's some
code for the user

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:38.000
interface to be able to
incorporate the consent here.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.020
And so first you can incorporate
the consent text via an HTML

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.640
document for example.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.150
And then you can use an
actionButton function

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:48.740
to create a nice
action button that

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:51.590
will either send the participant
to the study, if they agree,

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:55.780
or back to the home page if not.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:57.610
So on the server
side, we need to check

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:59.590
whether the user
clicked yes or no

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:02.280
and then send them
somewhere accordingly.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.760
Until this function will check.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.770
If the user clicked
yes, and if they did,

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:08.830
it's going to open a new window
and send them to the link.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:11.000
I'm doing this using renderUI.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.290
So I need to make sure to update
the user interface with that UI

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.727
output, so that this
actually works.

00:02:17.727 --> 00:02:19.310
And so that's what
this is doing here.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:21.430
This uiOutput and
then open_study

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:25.610
was the ID that I gave
that output in the server.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:29.080
So now that will actually
send them to the link.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.570
So one of the coolest things
about using Shiny for this

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.690
is that it gives you the power
of R from the web browser.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:37.750
So we can actually
randomize participants

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:39.160
at the time of consent.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:43.460
So it basically really
streamlines that process.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:46.757
So we just need to update the
server a little bit to do that.

00:02:46.757 --> 00:02:48.340
And so essentially
what I've done here

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.380
is, I've added a coin
flip to determine

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:53.920
which group the
participant should be in,

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:59.150
and then they're sent to a
different link accordingly.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:00.640
So we're developing
a package in R

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.590
that does this and a
few other neat features

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:06.310
like generates user IDs, saves
a randomization lists, allows

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.350
for unique links for
users, et cetera.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:12.160
It's very much under development
but you can find it on GitHub

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:13.760
at jdtrat/taskdesignr.

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:17.850
OK.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.860
So now that we
randomized folks, let's

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.700
collect some demographics.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.270
So we have another package
called shinysurveys,

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:26.270
that can help with this.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.478
And this one actually is
almost ready for prime time.

00:03:28.478 --> 00:03:30.180
So I encourage you to try it.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.530
You can install it from
GitHub at jdtrat/shinysurveys.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.050
And so basically what this
package allows you to do

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:43.010
is it lets you pass the data
frame with questions, options,

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:46.070
input types,
dependencies, et cetera.

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:47.600
And a nice Shiny
questionnaire is

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.120
going to be generated for you
that can then be incorporated

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.740
in your application.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.880
And so in R, it's as easy
as just loading the package

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.410
and then using the surveyOutput
function in the user interface,

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.510
passing the data frame
with your questions in it.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:07.315
And so here my data
frame is called d.

00:04:07.315 --> 00:04:11.420
And then using renderSurvey
in the server function

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:14.910
and you also will pass
it that same data frame.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.730
And this will automatically
generate a nice survey.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:19.490
It'll look something
like this, where

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:23.960
it has the questions as well as
the different places to input.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:27.230
You can have required questions
with those little stars.

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:28.760
You can also have dependencies.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:30.570
For example, if the
answer a certain way,

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:33.060
then new questions can
pop up accordingly.

00:04:35.724 --> 00:04:37.930
So finally all of this
can be incorporated

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:41.030
in commonly used shiny
tools like learnr modules.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:43.270
So for example,
learnr modules can

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:45.520
record all of the
user's input data, which

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.110
can be great for a study.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:49.990
And you can stick the user
IDs generated from the

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:51.190
taskdesignr package in here.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.340
And then you can save this
data with the unique ID

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.090
that can be linked to
the demographic data

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:57.190
for later analysis.

00:04:57.190 --> 00:04:57.820
So that's all.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:00.950
You can find me
@LucyStats on Twitter.

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:04.090
Thank you so much.

